<!-- Section 1: sec-intro -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Special Right Triangle Calculator</h2>
  <p>Solve 45-45-90 and 30-60-90 triangles from one known side, using their fixed side ratios.</p>
  <p>A special right triangle is a right triangle whose angles produce a fixed side ratio that never changes, no matter how large or small the triangle is. Only two angle patterns do this in school geometry: the 45-45-90 triangle and the 30-60-90 triangle. The Special Right Triangle Calculator on this page covers both types, so one known side is enough to find every other side.</p>
  <p>The 45-45-90 triangle has two equal legs, and its sides follow the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[a : b : c = 1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}]]')} />. The 30-60-90 triangle has three different side lengths, and its sides follow the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[a : b : c = 1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the legs and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse, and the meaning of <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> changes with the triangle type, so the sections below always state which type they use.</p>
  <p>If a 45-45-90 triangle has a leg of 5 cm, the other leg is also 5 cm and the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]')} /> cm. If a 30-60-90 triangle has a short leg of 5 cm, the long leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b = 5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm and the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm. The four calculator modes on this page perform exactly these steps, and the guide below explains where each ratio comes from, how to pick the right mode, and how to check each answer.</p>
</section>

