<!-- Section 3: sec-what -->
<section id="sec-what" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Are Special Right Triangles?</h2>
  <p>A special right triangle is a right triangle whose three angles are fixed at values that force its sides into one unchanging ratio. Most right triangles have no such shortcut: knowing one side tells you nothing about the others until you also know an angle or a second side. In a special right triangle, the angles alone fix the shape, so one side length fixes the whole triangle.</p>
  <p>The Pythagorean theorem still applies, and it is the tool that produces both ratios. In a 45-45-90 triangle, the two equal legs make the theorem collapse into <span set:html={rich('[[2a^2 = c^2]]')} />, which gives <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. In a 30-60-90 triangle, the hypotenuse is twice the short leg, so the theorem gives <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = (2a)^2 - a^2 = 3a^2]]')} />, which gives <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />. The full derivations appear in their own sections below.</p>
  <p>The ratios stay the same at every size because the triangles are similar within their own type. A 45-45-90 triangle with a 1 cm leg and one with a 100 cm leg have the same shape, the same angles, and the same 1:1:√2 ratio. That scale independence is what makes these triangles so useful in geometry and trigonometry: once you recognize the angle pattern, you can write down every side without measuring or using sine and cosine.</p>
  <p>The 45-45-90 and 30-60-90 triangles are two different families, not two names for the same shape. One has two equal legs and the other has three different side lengths. Confusing them is the most common error in this topic, so the next section introduces each family on its own before the guide compares them side by side.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('special-both-types.svg')} alt="Two special right triangles side by side: a 45-45-90 triangle with ratio 1:1:√2 and a 30-60-90 triangle with ratio 1:√3:2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The two special right triangles: 45-45-90 with equal legs, and 30-60-90 with three different sides.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-types -->
<section id="sec-types" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>The Two Main Types of Special Right Triangles</h2>
  <p>Both special triangles are right triangles, so each has a 90° angle and a hypotenuse opposite it. What differs is the pair of acute angles, and the acute angles decide the side ratio. Learn each type separately first, and the comparison later in this guide will be easy to follow.</p>
  <h3>45-45-90 Triangle</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Angles: 45°, 45°, 90°.</li>
    <li>Two equal legs, so either leg can serve as the known input.</li>
    <li>Hypotenuse equals the leg times √2, <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Side ratio: <span set:html={rich('[[a : b : c = 1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Also called an isosceles right triangle.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>30-60-90 Triangle</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Angles: 30°, 60°, 90°.</li>
    <li>Short leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> opposite the 30° angle, long leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> opposite the 60° angle.</li>
    <li>Hypotenuse equals twice the short leg, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Long leg equals the short leg times √3, <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Side ratio: <span set:html={rich('[[a : b : c = 1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} />.</li>
    <li>A scalene right triangle, since all three sides differ.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The visual difference is clear once you look for it. In a 45-45-90 triangle the two legs look the same length, and the hypotenuse runs at 45° to each leg. In a 30-60-90 triangle the leg opposite the 30° angle is visibly the shortest side, the leg opposite the 60° angle is longer, and the hypotenuse is exactly twice the short leg. The table below sums up the contrast.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Feature</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">45-45-90</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">30-60-90</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Angles</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">45°, 45°, 90°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">30°, 60°, 90°</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Side ratio</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1:1:√2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1:√3:2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Equal legs</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Yes</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">No</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Shortest side</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Both legs</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Short leg (opposite 30°)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Longest side</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse formula</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a√2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">2a</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Long leg formula</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Not applicable</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a√3</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Triangle type</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Isosceles right</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Scalene right</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: sec-454590 -->
<section id="sec-454590" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the 45-45-90 Triangle</h2>
  <p>A 45-45-90 triangle is an isosceles right triangle. Its two acute angles are both 45°, so the angles add to <span set:html={rich('[[45° + 45° + 90° = 180°]]')} />, as every triangle must. Because the two acute angles are equal, the sides opposite them are equal too, which is why the two legs have the same length.</p>
  <p>Call the legs <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. Equal legs mean <span set:html={rich('[[a = b]]')} />, and the side ratio is <span set:html={rich('[[a : b : c = 1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}]]')} />. The hypotenuse is the longest side, as in every right triangle, and it is longer than either leg by a factor of √2, about 1.414.</p>
  <p>The two working formulas are the hypotenuse from a leg and the leg from the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take a leg of 5 cm. The other leg is 5 cm, and the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]')} /> cm. The same steps work at any size: a leg of 12 cm gives a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[12\\sqrt{2} \\approx 16.971]]')} /> cm, and a leg of 1 unit gives a hypotenuse of √2 units. The <a href="/45-45-90-triangle-calculator/">45-45-90 triangle calculator</a> covers this triangle type in full detail, including area and perimeter.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('special-454590-geometry.svg')} alt="A 45-45-90 triangle with two equal legs marked congruent, angles 45°, 45°, 90°, and hypotenuse labeled a√2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">A 45-45-90 triangle: equal legs, equal acute angles, and a hypotenuse of a√2.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-derive-454590 -->
<section id="sec-derive-454590" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Deriving the 45-45-90 Side Ratio</h2>
  <p>The 1:1:√2 ratio is not a rule to memorize blindly. It follows in a few steps from the Pythagorean theorem, and seeing the steps once makes the ratio hard to forget. Start with the fact that the legs are equal.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = b]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Apply the Pythagorean theorem to the legs and the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Replace <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> with <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, since the legs are equal.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + a^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Combine the two identical terms.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[2a^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take the positive square root of both sides. Lengths are positive, so the negative root is dropped.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Set <span set:html={rich('[[a = 1]]')} /> and the ratio appears directly: legs 1 and 1, hypotenuse √2, so the ratio is <span set:html={rich('[[1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}]]')} />. The same ratio explains the diagonal of a square. A square with side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> has a diagonal that splits it into two congruent 45-45-90 triangles, and the diagonal is the hypotenuse of either one, so the diagonal equals <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. A square with an 8 cm side has a diagonal of <span set:html={rich('[[8\\sqrt{2} \\approx 11.314]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('special-454590-derivation.svg')} alt="A labeled 45-45-90 triangle showing the Pythagorean derivation a squared plus a squared equals c squared, giving c equals a times root 2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The Pythagorean derivation: equal legs turn a² + b² = c² into 2a² = c².</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('special-square-diagonal.svg')} alt="A square with side 8 cm and a diagonal of 8 root 2 cm, split into two congruent 45-45-90 triangles" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">A square diagonal splits the square into two congruent 45-45-90 triangles.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-306090 -->
<section id="sec-306090" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the 30-60-90 Triangle</h2>
  <p>A 30-60-90 triangle has three different angles, so it has three different side lengths. The side opposite the smallest angle is the shortest side, the side opposite the middle angle is the middle side, and the side opposite the 90° angle is the hypotenuse. That opposite-side rule is the whole key to labeling this triangle correctly.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> for the short leg opposite 30°, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> for the long leg opposite 60°, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> for the hypotenuse opposite 90°. The two working formulas are the long leg from the short leg and the hypotenuse from the short leg.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 2a]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The side ratio is <span set:html={rich('[[a : b : c = 1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} />, with √3 about 1.732. Take a short leg of 5 cm. The long leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b = 5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm and the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm. Check with the Pythagorean theorem: <span set:html={rich('[[5^2 + (5\\sqrt{3})^2 = 25 + 75 = 100 = 10^2]]')} />, which confirms the three sides fit together.</p>
  <p>The hypotenuse is always exactly twice the short leg, which is the fastest check in this topic. If a problem says the hypotenuse is 14 cm, the short leg is 7 cm at once, and the long leg follows as <span set:html={rich('[[7\\sqrt{3} \\approx 12.124]]')} /> cm. The <a href="/30-60-90-triangle-calculator/">30-60-90 triangle calculator</a> covers this triangle type in full detail, including its equilateral-triangle derivation.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('special-306090-geometry.svg')} alt="A 30-60-90 triangle with short leg a opposite 30 degrees, long leg a root 3 opposite 60 degrees, and hypotenuse 2a opposite 90 degrees" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">A 30-60-90 triangle: the short leg sits opposite 30°, the long leg opposite 60°.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-derive-306090 -->
<section id="sec-derive-306090" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Deriving the 30-60-90 Side Ratio</h2>
  <p>The 1:√3:2 ratio comes from an equilateral triangle, and the construction is worth following step by step. Start with an equilateral triangle whose side length is <span set:html={rich('[[2a]]')} />. All three of its angles are 60° and all three sides are equal.</p>
  <p>Draw an altitude from one vertex to the opposite side. The altitude does three things at once: it splits the equilateral triangle into two congruent right triangles, it cuts the base into two segments of length <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, and it splits one 60° angle into two 30° angles. Each half is now a triangle with angles 30°, 60°, and 90°.</p>
  <p>In either half, the hypotenuse is the original equilateral side, <span set:html={rich('[[2a]]')} />, and the short leg is the half-base, <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. Call the altitude, which is the long leg, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />. Apply the Pythagorean theorem to the half triangle.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b^2 = (2a)^2 - a^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Expand the square.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b^2 = 4a^2 - a^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Simplify.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b^2 = 3a^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take the positive square root.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Set <span set:html={rich('[[a = 1]]')} /> and the ratio appears: short leg 1, long leg √3, hypotenuse 2, so the ratio is <span set:html={rich('[[1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} />. The altitude of an equilateral triangle with side 10 cm is therefore <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm, since the altitude is the long leg of a 30-60-90 triangle with short leg 5 cm.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('special-equilateral-derivation.svg')} alt="An equilateral triangle with side 2a split by an altitude into two congruent 30-60-90 triangles, each with short leg a, long leg a root 3, and hypotenuse 2a" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">An altitude splits an equilateral triangle into two congruent 30-60-90 triangles.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-compare -->
<section id="sec-compare" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Comparing the Two Special Triangle Side Ratios</h2>
  <p>The two ratios look alike at a glance, which is exactly why they get mixed up. Both contain a square root and a small whole number, and both describe a right triangle. But the numbers sit in different places: in 1:1:√2 the root belongs to the hypotenuse, while in 1:√3:2 the root belongs to the long leg and the hypotenuse is the whole number 2.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Feature</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">45-45-90</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">30-60-90</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Angles</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">45°, 45°, 90°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">30°, 60°, 90°</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Side ratio</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1:1:√2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1:√3:2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Equal legs</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Yes</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">No</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Shortest side</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Both legs</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Short leg</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Longest side</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse formula</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a√2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">2a</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Long leg formula</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Not applicable</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a√3</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Triangle type</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Isosceles right</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Scalene right</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The formulas cannot be interchanged. Multiplying a 30-60-90 short leg by √2 gives a wrong hypotenuse, and doubling a 45-45-90 leg gives a wrong hypotenuse. The two triangles come from different constructions, a square diagonal for 45-45-90 and an equilateral triangle altitude for 30-60-90, so their ratios answer different geometry questions.</p>
  <p>One numerical example for each type shows the gap. A 45-45-90 triangle with leg 5 cm has hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]')} /> cm. A 30-60-90 triangle with short leg 5 cm has hypotenuse 10 cm and long leg <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm. Same starting number, very different answers, and the only way to tell them apart is the angle pattern.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('special-ratio-comparison.svg')} alt="Side-by-side comparison of the 45-45-90 ratio 1:1:√2 and the 30-60-90 ratio 1:√3:2 at matching scale" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Both ratios at matching scale: the root sits on the hypotenuse for 45-45-90, on the long leg for 30-60-90.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-how-hyp-45 -->
<section id="sec-how-hyp-45" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the 45-45-90 Hypotenuse Calculator</h2>
  <p>Mode 1 is the most used mode on this page, and it takes only a few seconds once the triangle type is confirmed. Follow the steps in order.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Confirm the triangle is 45-45-90: both acute angles measure 45°.</li>
    <li>Pick either equal leg as the known side, since both legs have the same length.</li>
    <li>Select the 45-45-90 hypotenuse mode in the calculator.</li>
    <li>Enter the leg length as a positive number.</li>
    <li>Choose the unit that matches the rest of the problem.</li>
    <li>Click Calculate Hypotenuse c.</li>
    <li>Read the result, which is the hypotenuse in the same unit.</li>
    <li>Use Reset before starting a new calculation.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The mode applies the formula below.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With a leg of 7 cm, the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 7\\sqrt{2} \\approx 9.899]]')} /> cm. The formula is valid only when both acute angles are 45°, so check the angles before trusting the answer. If the angles are 30° and 60° instead, switch to a 30-60-90 mode.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-how-leg-45 -->
<section id="sec-how-leg-45" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find a Leg From the 45-45-90 Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>Mode 2 works backward from the hypotenuse to a leg. It divides by √2, which is the step most often forgotten. The formula is shown below.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The same result can be written in rationalized form, which avoids a root in the denominator.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\frac{c\\sqrt{2}}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>To use the mode, confirm the 45-45-90 angle pattern, select the 45-45-90 leg mode, enter the hypotenuse, choose the unit, and click Calculate. With a hypotenuse of 10 cm, the leg is <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{2}} = 5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]')} /> cm. Both legs have this length, since the legs are equal, so the one answer describes the whole triangle. A quick check: <span set:html={rich('[[7.071^2 + 7.071^2 \\approx 50 + 50 = 100 = 10^2]]')} />, which matches the hypotenuse squared.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-how-long-30 -->
<section id="sec-how-long-30" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the 30-60-90 Long Leg Calculator</h2>
  <p>Mode 3 finds the long leg from the short leg, and the only hard part is identifying the short leg correctly. The short leg is the side opposite the 30° angle, and it is always the shortest side of the triangle.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Confirm the triangle is 30-60-90 from its angles.</li>
    <li>Find the side opposite the 30° angle: that is the short leg.</li>
    <li>Select the 30-60-90 long leg mode in the calculator.</li>
    <li>Enter the short leg as a positive number.</li>
    <li>Choose the unit that matches the rest of the problem.</li>
    <li>Click Calculate Long Leg.</li>
    <li>Read the result, which is the long leg in the same unit.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The mode applies the formula below.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With a short leg of 6 cm, the long leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b = 6\\sqrt{3} \\approx 10.392]]')} /> cm. Entering the hypotenuse or the long leg by mistake gives a wrong answer, so label the sides first and enter the short leg only.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-how-hyp-30 -->
<section id="sec-how-hyp-30" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the 30-60-90 Hypotenuse Calculator</h2>
  <p>Mode 4 is the simplest mode on the page, because the hypotenuse of a 30-60-90 triangle is exactly twice the short leg. There is no square root involved, which makes the answer easy to check by hand.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Confirm the triangle is 30-60-90 from its angles.</li>
    <li>Identify the short leg, the side opposite the 30° angle.</li>
    <li>Select the 30-60-90 hypotenuse mode in the calculator.</li>
    <li>Enter the short leg as a positive number.</li>
    <li>Choose the unit that matches the rest of the problem.</li>
    <li>Click Calculate Hypotenuse c.</li>
    <li>Read the result, which is the hypotenuse in the same unit.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The mode applies the formula below.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 2a]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With a short leg of 8 cm, the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 16]]')} /> cm. The long leg for the same triangle is <span set:html={rich('[[8\\sqrt{3} \\approx 13.856]]')} /> cm, which sits between the short leg and the hypotenuse, as it should. If a result ever comes out smaller than the short leg, the wrong mode was selected.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples for Both Special Triangle Types</h2>
  <p>These two worked examples solve every side of each triangle type from one known side. Area and perimeter appear here as extra manual calculations, not as calculator modes: the calculator on this page finds side lengths only.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: 45-45-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given a 45-45-90 triangle with leg <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, find the other leg, the hypotenuse, the area, and the perimeter.</p>
  <p>The legs are equal, so the other leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b = 5]]')} /> cm. The hypotenuse follows from the ratio.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The area is half the product of the legs.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{25}{2} = 12.5 \\text{ cm}^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The perimeter is the sum of all three sides.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = 10 + 5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 17.071 \\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <h3>Example 2: 30-60-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given a 30-60-90 triangle with short leg <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, find the long leg, the hypotenuse, the area, and the perimeter.</p>
  <p>The long leg is the short leg times √3, and the hypotenuse is twice the short leg.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = 5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 10]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The area is half the product of the legs.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\approx 21.651 \\text{ cm}^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The perimeter is the sum of all three sides.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = 15 + 5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 23.660 \\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Both examples check out with the Pythagorean theorem. For the first, <span set:html={rich('[[5^2 + 5^2 = 50]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[7.071^2 \\approx 50]]')} />. For the second, <span set:html={rich('[[5^2 + 8.660^2 \\approx 25 + 75 = 100 = 10^2]]')} />.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-area-perimeter -->
<section id="sec-area-perimeter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Area and Perimeter Relationships in Special Right Triangles</h2>
  <p>Area and perimeter are useful follow-up calculations once the sides are known, but they are manual relationships, not functions of the calculator on this page. The formulas below use the same side labels as the rest of the guide, with <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> as the known leg for 45-45-90 and the short leg for 30-60-90.</p>
  <p>For a 45-45-90 triangle, the area is half the square of a leg, and the perimeter is the leg times <span set:html={rich('[[2 + \\sqrt{2}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{a^2}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = a(2 + \\sqrt{2})]]]')} /></p>
  <p>For a 30-60-90 triangle, the area is half the product of the legs, and the perimeter is the short leg times <span set:html={rich('[[3 + \\sqrt{3}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{a^2\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = a(3 + \\sqrt{3})]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take a 45-45-90 triangle with leg 6 cm. Its area is <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{36}{2} = 18]]')} /> cm², and its perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[P = 6(2 + \\sqrt{2}) \\approx 20.485]]')} /> cm. Take a 30-60-90 triangle with short leg 6 cm. Its area is <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{36\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 18\\sqrt{3} \\approx 31.177]]')} /> cm², and its perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[P = 6(3 + \\sqrt{3}) \\approx 28.392]]')} /> cm. Keep the units straight: area is always in square units and perimeter in linear units, so an answer of 18 cm for an area is a unit error, not a rounding error.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-exact -->
<section id="sec-exact" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Exact Radical Answers Versus Decimal Approximations</h2>
  <p>The square roots in this guide are irrational numbers, which means their decimal expansions never end and never repeat. The two values to know are shown below.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.41421356]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.73205081]]]')} /></p>
  <p>An exact answer keeps the root in place, such as <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, while a decimal answer replaces it with an approximation, such as <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]')} />. Exact answers are preferred in school mathematics because they carry no rounding error and they show the structure of the problem. A teacher can see at once that <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> came from a 45-45-90 triangle, while 7.071 could have come from anywhere.</p>
  <p>Decimals are better for practical measurements, where a ruler or a cutting tool needs a number, not a symbol. When a decimal is needed, round only at the end of the calculation, never in the middle. Rounding √2 to 1.41 before multiplying by 5 gives 7.05, which is off by 0.021 from the true 7.071, and the error grows with larger numbers. Label every decimal with the approximately equal sign, so a reader never mistakes <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> for an exact equality.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify Special Right Triangle Calculations</h2>
  <p>Every special triangle answer can be checked in under a minute with three tests: the side ratio, the Pythagorean theorem, and the angle sum. Run at least two of them before trusting a result.</p>
  <p>For a 45-45-90 triangle, check that the legs are equal, <span set:html={rich('[[a = b]]')} />, and that the sides satisfy the Pythagorean theorem, <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 7.071]]')} /> cm, the check is <span set:html={rich('[[25 + 25 = 50]]')} /> against <span set:html={rich('[[7.071^2 \\approx 50]]')} />, which agrees.</p>
  <p>For a 30-60-90 triangle, check that the long leg is the short leg times √3, <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />, that the hypotenuse is twice the short leg, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />, and that <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8.660]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, the check is <span set:html={rich('[[25 + 75 = 100 = 10^2]]')} />, which agrees.</p>
  <p>Also verify the angle sum: the three angles must add to 180°. A 45-45-90 triangle gives <span set:html={rich('[[45° + 45° + 90° = 180°]]')} />, and a 30-60-90 triangle gives <span set:html={rich('[[30° + 60° + 90° = 180°]]')} />. If the ratio, the theorem, and the angles all agree, the answer is solid. If any one of them fails, the triangle type was identified wrong or the wrong formula was used.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Solving Special Right Triangles</h2>
  <p>Fourteen mistakes cause nearly every wrong answer in this topic. Each one is listed below with the correct approach, so you can spot your own error fast.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Confusing 45-45-90 with 30-60-90 triangles. Check the angles first: two 45° angles mean 45-45-90, while 30° and 60° mean 30-60-90.</li>
    <li>Using √3 instead of √2. The 45-45-90 hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, never <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Using √2 instead of √3. The 30-60-90 long leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />, never <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Assuming both legs are equal in a 30-60-90 triangle. Only the 45-45-90 triangle has equal legs; in 30-60-90 the long leg is √3 times the short leg.</li>
    <li>Assuming the short and long legs are equal. The short leg sits opposite 30° and the long leg opposite 60°, so they differ by a factor of √3.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that the 30-60-90 hypotenuse equals twice the short leg. It is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} />, a whole-number doubling with no square root.</li>
    <li>Using the wrong input side. Mode 3 and Mode 4 both need the short leg, so entering the long leg or the hypotenuse gives a wrong answer.</li>
    <li>Confusing the short leg with the hypotenuse. The hypotenuse is the longest side and sits opposite the 90° angle; the short leg is the shortest side.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to divide by √2 when finding a 45-45-90 leg. The leg is <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{2}}]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[a = c\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, which would make the leg longer than the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to multiply by √3 when finding a 30-60-90 long leg. The long leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />, and using <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> alone gives the short leg back.</li>
    <li>Rounding radicals too early. Keep <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> exact through every step and round only the final answer.</li>
    <li>Mixing units. A leg in cm and a hypotenuse in mm cannot share one formula; convert first, then calculate.</li>
    <li>Confusing area and perimeter. Area uses square units and the formula <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a^2}{2}]]')} /> for 45-45-90, while perimeter uses linear units and <span set:html={rich('[[P = a(2 + \\sqrt{2})]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Applying special triangle formulas to arbitrary right triangles. The ratios 1:1:√2 and 1:√3:2 work only for their own angle patterns; a 20-70-90 triangle needs trigonometry instead.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Applications of Special Right Triangles</h2>
  <p>Special right triangles show up wherever squares, equilateral triangles, and standard angles appear. A square diagonal is a 45-45-90 problem: a square tabletop with a 60 cm side has a diagonal of <span set:html={rich('[[60\\sqrt{2} \\approx 84.853]]')} /> cm. An equilateral triangle altitude is a 30-60-90 problem: a triangular sign with a 40 cm side has an altitude of <span set:html={rich('[[20\\sqrt{3} \\approx 34.641]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>In class, these triangles make geometry homework and trigonometry exercises faster, because standard angles like 30°, 45°, and 60° have exact sine and cosine values that come straight from these ratios. In technical drawing and standard-angle drafting, 45° and 30-60 set squares are built on exactly these triangles, so a drafter can lay out a 45° line or a 30° line knowing the side proportions in advance.</p>
  <p>Architecture and design concepts use the same patterns for diagonal bracing, roof pitches, and tile layouts, and construction geometry uses them for quick on-site checks, such as confirming a corner is square by measuring a 3-4-5 style diagonal or a 45-45-90 diagonal. Mathematical modeling and classroom demonstrations use them too, since the exact radical answers keep the reasoning visible instead of hiding it inside a calculator. Treat these as geometry shortcuts for planning and checking, not as a substitute for proper engineering measurement where precision is required.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 20: sec-practice -->
<section id="sec-practice" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practice Problems With Answers</h2>
  <p>Work each problem on your own, then check the solution. Area and perimeter questions are manual exercises, not calculator modes. Use different numbers from the main examples so the patterns have to be applied fresh.</p>
  <h3>45-45-90 Problems</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>A 45-45-90 triangle has a leg of 7 cm. Find the hypotenuse. Solution: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 7\\sqrt{2} \\approx 9.899]]')} /> cm.</li>
